<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phrygia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phrygia was a region of west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, named after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>, who formed a kingdom there in the early first millennium BCE with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a> and kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>. After the Cimmerian invasions it came under Lydian, Persian, Hellenistic and then Roman rule. Greater Phrygia, the inland part, was briefly granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-v-of-pontus">Mithridates V of Pontus</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the late second century BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phrygia.jpg" alt="Phrygia" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amizzoni · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkey_ancient_region_map_phrygia.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Phrygia lay on the western end of the high Anatolian plateau, an arid region quite unlike the forested lands to its north and west, centred on the Sangarios, or Sakarya, River. The climate is harsh, with hot summers and cold winters, and the land is used mostly for livestock grazing and barley. In the north-west lay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorylaeum">Dorylaeum</a> and the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a>; to the south stood Midas City (Yazılıkaya), Akroinon with its marble quarries at Docimium, and Synnada, and at the western end Aizanoi and Acmonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greek tradition held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> had migrated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says they were called Bryges. Their language, which survived until the 6th century CE, was Indo-European and had some vocabulary like Greek, and was not an Anatolian language like Hittite. Some scholars treat the migration as legend and place the Phrygians' arrival around the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> at the start of the 12th century BCE, while others seek their origins among peoples already subject to the Hittites.</p><p>In the 8th century BCE the kingdom, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> in the upper Sakarya valley, grew into an empire dominating most of central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and bordering Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and Urartu. Its last independent king was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> sacked Gordium around 695 BCE. Lydia expelled the Cimmerians by about 620 BCE and absorbed much of Phrygia, and in the 540s BCE it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Lydia. Darius the Great made the Persian satrapy of Phrygia in the west with its capital at Dascylium, and during the 5th century the region was split into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespontine-phrygia">Hellespontine Phrygia</a> and Greater Phrygia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> cut the Gordian Knot at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> in 333 BCE. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> overran northern Phrygia, which became part of Galatia, and destroyed Gordium. In 188 BCE the southern remnant came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, and in 133 BCE it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Diocletian divided it into Phrygia Salutaris, with Synnada as capital, and Pacatiana, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laodicea-on-the-lycus">Laodicea on the Lycus</a>. The Turks overran it after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071, and the name remained in use until the Ottoman conquest of 1453.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Greek myth told of Phrygian kings: Gordias, whose knot Alexander cut, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midas">Midas</a>, who turned what he touched to gold, and Mygdon, who warred with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazons</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a> are close allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>. Phrygian religion was headed by a goddess, Matar Kubeleya, the Mother of the Mountain Peaks. Over the centuries the Phrygians became Christian and Greek-speaking and assimilated into the Byzantine state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Phrygia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed <b>Phrygia</b> since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to Syria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Assyrian records call him Gugu of Luddi and report that he sought the help of Ashurbanipal against the Cimmerians, nomadic invaders from beyond the Caucasus who had destroyed the Kingdom of <b>Phrygia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Phrygia</b> occupied the western end of the high Anatolian plateau, an arid land of hot summers and cold winters where olives will not easily grow and the ground is given to grazing and barley.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…to secure their position: Mithridates II married a Seleucid princess, Mithridates V sent help to Rome in the Third Punic War and against Aristonicus in Asia and was rewarded with <b>Phrygia</b>, which Rome later withdrew, and Mithridates VI married his daughter Cleopatra to Tigranes the Great of Armenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The rivalries of Assyria, Urartu, <b>Phrygia</b> and smaller states offered rich opportunities, and the Cimmerians served whoever paid them best.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>A cult found in Thrace, Moesia, <b>Phrygia</b> and the lands of the Dacians and Getae honoured the “Thracian horseman”, also known as Sabazios or the Thracian Heros, by a Thracian name Heros Karabazmos, a god of the underworld usually shown on funeral monuments as a rider slaying a…</blockquote><a href="/culture/thracians">Thracians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
